# Forensic Audit Report: Pennsylvania ACA Individual and Small Group Market, Plan Year 2027

## Executive Summary

Regulation and actuarial evaluation of the Affordable Care Act (ACA) individual and small group health insurance markets in the Commonwealth of Pennsylvania require rigorous oversight to ensure rate equity, solvency, benefit compliance, and seamless consumer auto-enrollment. This forensic audit report evaluates the statutory, financial, and structural framework for Plan Year 2027 (PY 2027), utilizing data verified within the Pennsylvania ACA Market Plan Year 2027 Complete Forensic Audit Package (Version 2.0, Final Verified). The analysis encompasses state-specific regulatory mandates enforced by the Pennsylvania Insurance Department (PID), federal oversight conducted by the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS), and market operations administered through Pennie, Pennsylvania's State-Based Exchange (SBE).

The audit evaluates four critical pillars of PY 2027 market operations: (a) geographic rating area integrity, (b) single risk pool actuarial compliance, (c) Plan ID Crosswalk automated mapping logic, and (d) plan cost-sharing taxonomy. A central finding is the compound stress exerted on the single risk pool following the expiration of enhanced federal premium tax credits at the end of calendar year 2025. This structural shift produced an unsubsidized premium shock in PY 2026 (average gross rate increase of 21.5%; [Pennsylvania Insurance Department, 2026](https://www.insurance.pa.gov)) and sustained rate pressure for PY 2027 (requested average increase of approximately 16.0%; [Pennsylvania Insurance Department, 2026](https://www.insurance.pa.gov)). Mid-year market attrition reached unprecedented levels in late 2026, with active enrollment falling from a peak open enrollment headcount of 501,459 to 417,158 enrollees by September 2026 (a net loss of 84,301 enrollees, or −16.8%; see *Calculation Subsection 1*).

Understanding the second- and third-order impacts of these macro-level affordability shocks is essential. Higher unsubsidized premiums alter enrollee distribution across actuarial value (AV) metal tiers, driving price-sensitive, lower-risk individuals out of the market or into Bronze-tier options. This adverse selection tilt destabilizes the underlying single risk pool, forcing issuers to recalibrate claims trends and morbidity assumptions in their Unified Rate Review Template (URRT) submissions. Simultaneously, strict CMS Plan ID Crosswalk rules under [45 CFR § 155.335(j)](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-45/section-155.335) require precise mapping of discontinued plans to prevent coverage disruption, while requiring issuers to maintain strict network ID alignment to mitigate out-of-network provider exposure for passively re-enrolled members.

