## Pennie Marketplace Dynamics, Risk Pool Stability, and Demographic Profiling

Pennie, Pennsylvania's State-Based Exchange created under state legislation in 2019, serves as the central portal for individual health insurance shopping, financial assistance determination, and enrollment. Since transitioning off the Federal Platform (Healthcare.gov), Pennie expanded coverage to over 500,000 enrolled individuals during the PY 2026 Open Enrollment Period. Pennie operates in close coordination with the Pennsylvania Insurance Department to administer federal Advanced Premium Tax Credits (APTC) and Cost-Sharing Reductions (CSR).

The market evolution of Pennie enrollment reflects steady growth followed by severe post-subsidy recalibration. During its initial operational year in PY 2021, Pennie covered approximately 330,000 enrollees. Expansion of federal subsidy caps under the American Rescue Plan Act of 2021 ([Pub. L. 117-2](https://www.congress.gov/bill/117th-congress/house-bill/537)) and the Inflation Reduction Act of 2022 ([Pub. L. 117-169](https://www.congress.gov/bill/117th-congress/house-bill/537)) boosted enrollment to over 430,000 enrollees by PY 2024, reaching an all-time peak of 501,459 enrollees during the PY 2026 Open Enrollment Period. However, as unsubsidized gross premiums escalated by an average of 21.5%, lower-income and middle-income enrollees faced significantly higher monthly premium contributions. By September 2026, total active enrollment dropped to 417,158 enrollees. This net loss of over 84,000 enrollees represents an elevated attrition rate compared to historical SBE trends (see Calculation Subsection 3).

