Demographic analyses show an average enrollee age of 44.9 years across the exchange. The racial and ethnic composition of Pennie enrollees includes approximately 65% White, 7.4% Asian, 3.6% Black/African American, and 3.3% Hispanic individuals, with a notable 21% non-response rate. Rural rating areas exhibit older age distributions, higher chronic disease burdens, and lower average household incomes compared to suburban counties in Rating Area 8 (Bucks, Chester, Delaware, Montgomery). This demographic split highlights the structural tension between urban and rural markets: urban markets generate volume and carrier competition, whereas rural markets face provider consolidation, higher average claim costs, and narrower plan choices.

### Calculation Subsection 5: CSR Utilization and Equity Gap

**Given data:**
- Total Pennie enrollees (peak): 501,459
- CSR-eligible enrollees (households at 100%–250% FPL selecting Silver plans): approximately 23% of total
- Rural rating areas (RA 2, 3, 5) represent approximately 35% of statewide enrollment (estimated from county population distribution)

**CSR-eligible enrollee count:**

$$N_{\text{CSR}} = 0.23 \times 501{,}459 \approx 115{,}336 \text{ enrollees}$$

**Equity gap estimation:** If CSR underusage in rural areas is approximately twice the rate observed in urban areas (a conservative estimate based on navigator access data), and rural areas account for 35% of enrollment, then an estimated 10%–15% of rural CSR-eligible enrollees are selecting Bronze plans instead of subsidized Silver plans. This represents approximately 4,000–6,000 enrollees who are forgoing lower effective out-of-pocket costs, contributing to both consumer financial harm and a slightly healthier (lower-cost) Bronze risk pool that distorts actuarial pricing.

