## Strategic Regulatory Recommendations

The forensic audit of the Pennsylvania ACA individual and small group market for Plan Year 2027 demonstrates structural compliance with core federal and state mandates. Geographic rating areas strictly enforce statutory single risk pool rules under [45 CFR § 156.80](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-45/section-156.80), rate filings adhere to PID guidance via SERFF, and CMS Plan ID Crosswalk templates follow auto-enrollment rules under [45 CFR § 155.335(j)](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-45/section-155.335). However, the post-2025 federal subsidy expiration introduced market volatility, driving premium increases (+21.5% in PY 2026; ≈16.0% in PY 2027) and elevated enrollee attrition.

To strengthen marketplace stability, enhance actuarial equity, and maintain coverage continuity for Plan Year 2027 and beyond, regulatory oversight bodies should implement the following targeted policy actions:

- **Enhance single risk pool morbidity auditing.** The Pennsylvania Insurance Department should mandate detailed actuarial disclosures within URRT submissions to evaluate post-subsidy morbidity shifts. Actuaries must explicitly isolate morbidity adjustment factors from medical inflation trends to prevent unwarranted rate adjustments.
- **Deploy targeted crosswalk auto-enrollment communications.** Pennie and participating carriers must deploy proactive consumer outreach targeting passively re-enrolled individuals. Enrollees crosswalked into plans experiencing rate increases above 10% should receive clear notices highlighting lower-cost alternative plans within their rating area to reduce mid-year attrition.
- **Address rural cost-sharing reduction underusage.** Pennie should implement localized educational campaigns in Rating Areas 2, 3, and 5 to address CSR underusage. Navigators and certified brokers should guide low-income enrollees toward Silver-tier plans to maximize federal cost-sharing subsidies.
- **Enforce network integrity in plan crosswalking.** The PID and CMS should strictly audit issuer Plan ID Crosswalk templates to ensure high provider network overlap for discontinued plans. Mappings that alter primary provider networks must be flagged for regulatory review to prevent care disruption.

